  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 PM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Another round of fog and low clouds during the \nMonday morning commute.  \n\nSimilar to this morning, and thanks to ongoing light wind, high \ndwpts and minimal dwpt depressions, much of central NC will see a \nfew hours of locally dense fog and low clouds during the Monday \nmorning rush hour commute.  The highest dwpts and thus best chance \nfor said fog will be east, so look for best coverage of dense fog \nmainly to the south and east of Raleigh. Similar to this morning, \nthe fog should burn off by 9:30 AM.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... High heat and humidity return quickly this week, \nbeginning Monday and peaking Wednesday through Friday.\n\nAloft, the ~595 dam mid-level anticyclone will build eastward into \nand settle over the region through at least mid-week. Disturbances \nin the northern stream may help push the ridge south and west toward \nthe end of the week, with the trough amplifying across the East \nCoast over the weekend. At the surface, a lee trough may remain in \nplace across the area, while Bermuda high pressure ridges wwd across \nthe region, with a sly component to the wind across much of the area \nthrough at least mid-week. The trough may amplify across the region \nlate-week. Low level thicknesses of 1440+ m expected across the area \nWed-Fri. Temperatures should moderate through mid-week, with highs \nin the mid/upr 90s for Wed-Fri (10+ degrees above normal). Some \nlocations could reach 100 degrees Wed and Thu. Lows in the 70s \nexpected through much of the week. Heat index values of 100-110 are \npossible across much of the area Tue-Fri, highest Wed and Thu. Given \nthe prolonged period of heat with little relief overnight, the WPC \nExperimental Heat Risk continues to show Major to Extreme risk of \nheat-related impacts across central NC Tue-Fri. Heat advisories may \nbe needed should the forecast hold. Expect largely dry weather \nthrough Thu, though a stray shower/storm during the aft/eve cannot \nbe completely ruled out. Chances for storms increase toward the \nweekend as the mid/upr trough amplifies over the region.",
